#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532947 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨-6,3,-4⟩$, $b=⟨k,3,-3⟩$, and $c=⟨-4,8,7⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 3 & -4 \\\\ k & 3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((-6)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{vmatrix})+((-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & -3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{vmatrix})+((-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((-6)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (7)-(-3)\\cdot (8)))+((-3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (7)-(-3)\\cdot (-4)))+((-4)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (8)-(3)\\cdot (-4)))\\\\ &=((-6)\\cdot (45))+((-3)\\cdot (7k-12))+((-4)\\cdot (8k+12))\\\\ &=(-270)+(36-21k)+(-32k-48)\\\\ &=-53k-282\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-53k-282&=0\\\\ -53k&=282\\\\ k&=-\\frac{282}{53}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{282}{53}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532948 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨1,0,-1⟩$, $b=⟨2,-2,0⟩$, and $c=⟨1,-2,k⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & k\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{vmatrix})+((0)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix})+((-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (k)-(0)\\cdot (-2)))+((0)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (k)-(0)\\cdot (1)))+((-1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot (1)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (-2k))+((0)\\cdot (2k))+((-1)\\cdot (-2))\\\\ &=(-2k)+(0)+(2)\\\\ &=2-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2k&=0\\\\ -2k&=-2\\\\ k&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532950 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨-5,-5,3⟩$, $b=⟨k,-8,4⟩$, and $c=⟨-3,-7,7⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -5 & 3 \\\\ k & -8 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -7 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((-5)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{vmatrix})+((5)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix})+((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & -8 \\\\ -3 & -7\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((-5)\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (7)-(4)\\cdot (-7)))+((5)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (7)-(4)\\cdot (-3)))+((3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-7)-(-8)\\cdot (-3)))\\\\ &=((-5)\\cdot (-28))+((5)\\cdot (7k+12))+((3)\\cdot (-7k-24))\\\\ &=(140)+(35k+60)+(-21k-72)\\\\ &=14k+128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14k+128&=0\\\\ 14k&=-128\\\\ k&=-\\frac{64}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{64}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532952 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨0,-1,-1⟩$, $b=⟨2,0,1⟩$, and $c=⟨k,0,-2⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ k & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix})+((1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix})+((-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ k & 0\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-2)-(1)\\cdot (0)))+((1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(1)\\cdot (k)))+((-1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (0)-(0)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (0))+((1)\\cdot (-k-4))+((-1)\\cdot (0))\\\\ &=(0)+(-k-4)+(0)\\\\ &=-k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k-4&=0\\\\ -k&=4\\\\ k&=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532955 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨2,2,0⟩$, $b=⟨3,0,k⟩$, and $c=⟨-3,-1,-2⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & k \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix})+((-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & k \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix})+((0)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-2)-(k)\\cdot (-1)))+((-2)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (-2)-(k)\\cdot (-3)))+((0)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (-1)-(0)\\cdot (-3)))\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot (k))+((-2)\\cdot (3k-6))+((0)\\cdot (-3))\\\\ &=(2k)+(12-6k)+(0)\\\\ &=12-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4k&=0\\\\ -4k&=-12\\\\ k&=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532959 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨1,-3,-3⟩$, $b=⟨k,0,-2⟩$, and $c=⟨-2,-2,0⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ k & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix})+((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix})+((-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)))+((3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (0)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)))+((-3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-2)-(0)\\cdot (-2)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (-4))+((3)\\cdot (-4))+((-3)\\cdot (-2k))\\\\ &=(-4)+(-12)+(6k)\\\\ &=6k-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k-16&=0\\\\ 6k&=16\\\\ k&=\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{8}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532963 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨3,-3,5⟩$, $b=⟨-8,8,k⟩$, and $c=⟨-2,5,-8⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -8 & 8 & k \\\\ -2 & 5 & -8\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & k \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{vmatrix})+((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-8 & k \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{vmatrix})+((5)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot ((8)\\cdot (-8)-(k)\\cdot (5)))+((3)\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (-8)-(k)\\cdot (-2)))+((5)\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (5)-(8)\\cdot (-2)))\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot (-5k-64))+((3)\\cdot (2k+64))+((5)\\cdot (-24))\\\\ &=(-15k-192)+(6k+192)+(-120)\\\\ &=-9k-120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9k-120&=0\\\\ -9k&=120\\\\ k&=-\\frac{40}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{40}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532964 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨6,-7,4⟩$, $b=⟨2,-2,7⟩$, and $c=⟨k,-2,-3⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 7 \\\\ k & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix})+((7)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ k & -3\\end{vmatrix})+((4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-3)-(7)\\cdot (-2)))+((7)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-3)-(7)\\cdot (k)))+((4)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot (20))+((7)\\cdot (-7k-6))+((4)\\cdot (2k-4))\\\\ &=(120)+(-49k-42)+(8k-16)\\\\ &=62-41k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}62-41k&=0\\\\ -41k&=-62\\\\ k&=\\frac{62}{41}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{62}{41}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533019 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=2 xy + x - y$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 4.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2 uv + u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 uv + u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2 v + 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 uv + u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2 u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 v + 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 v + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 v + 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 2 v - 1,\\,1 - 2 u,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2 uv + u - v} \\\\  & =⟨2 (2 uv + u - v),\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4 uv + 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨4 uv + 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\cdot ⟨- 2 v - 1,\\,1 - 2 u,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(4 uv + 2 u - 2 v)\\cdot (- 2 v - 1)+(2 uv)\\cdot (1 - 2 u)+(2 u + 2 v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 8 uv^{2} - 8 uv - 2 u + 4 v^{2} + 2 v- 4 u^{2} v + 2 uv+2 u + 2 v \\\\  & =- 4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv + 4 v^{2} + 4 v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv + 4 v^{2} + 4 v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(- 4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv + 4 v^{2} + 4 v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533020 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=- xy - x - y$ for $-3\\le x\\le 4$ and $-2\\le y\\le 4.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{-3}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,- uv - u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 4,\\,-2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,- v - 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & - v - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & - u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & - v - 1 \\\\ 1 & - u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - v - 1 \\\\ 0 & - u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v + 1,\\,u + 1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=- uv - u - v} \\\\  & =⟨(- uv - u - v),\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- uv - u - v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨- uv - u - v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\cdot ⟨v + 1,\\,u + 1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(- uv - u - v)\\cdot (v + 1)+(2 uv)\\cdot (u + 1)+(2 u + 2 v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- uv^{2} - 2 uv - u - v^{2} - v+2 u^{2} v + 2 uv+2 u + 2 v \\\\  & =2 u^{2} v - uv^{2} + u - v^{2} + v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2 u^{2} v - uv^{2} + u - v^{2} + v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(2 u^{2} v - uv^{2} + u - v^{2} + v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533024 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x + 2 y,\\,- x + y,\\,2 - z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x^{2} + y^{2}$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 4.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u^{2} + v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 u \\\\ 1 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 u \\\\ 0 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 2 u,\\,- 2 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x + 2 y,\\,- x + y,\\,2 - z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u^{2} + v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,- u + v,\\,2 - (u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,- u + v,\\,- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,- u + v,\\,- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2⟩\\cdot ⟨- 2 u,\\,- 2 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u + 2 v)\\cdot (- 2 u)+(- u + v)\\cdot (- 2 v)+(- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 2 u^{2} - 4 uv+2 uv - 2 v^{2}- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2 \\\\  & =- 3 u^{2} - 2 uv - 3 v^{2} + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x + 2 y,\\,- x + y,\\,2 - z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 3 u^{2} - 2 uv - 3 v^{2} + 2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}(- 3 u^{2} - 2 uv - 3 v^{2} + 2)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533026 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,x + y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=- 2 xy - x - y$ for $1\\le x\\le 4$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,- 2 uv - u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 2 uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,- 2 v - 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 2 uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- 2 u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & - 2 v - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & - 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & - 2 v - 1 \\\\ 1 & - 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - 2 v - 1 \\\\ 0 & - 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v + 1,\\,2 u + 1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,x + y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=- 2 uv - u - v} \\\\  & =⟨2 (- 2 uv - u - v),\\,2 uv,\\,u + v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 4 uv - 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,u + v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨- 4 uv - 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨2 v + 1,\\,2 u + 1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(- 4 uv - 2 u - 2 v)\\cdot (2 v + 1)+(2 uv)\\cdot (2 u + 1)+(u + v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 8 uv^{2} - 8 uv - 2 u - 4 v^{2} - 2 v+4 u^{2} v + 2 uv+u + v \\\\  & =4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv - u - 4 v^{2} - v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,x + y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv - u - 4 v^{2} - v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv - u - 4 v^{2} - v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533035 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,xy,\\,x + y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=2 xy - x - y$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2 uv - u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2 v - 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2 u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 v - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 v - 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 v - 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1 - 2 v,\\,1 - 2 u,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,xy,\\,x + y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2 uv - u - v} \\\\  & =⟨(2 uv - u - v),\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 uv - u - v,\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨2 uv - u - v,\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨1 - 2 v,\\,1 - 2 u,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(2 uv - u - v)\\cdot (1 - 2 v)+(uv)\\cdot (1 - 2 u)+(u + v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 4 uv^{2} + 4 uv - u + 2 v^{2} - v- 2 u^{2} v + uv+u + v \\\\  & =- 2 u^{2} v - 4 uv^{2} + 5 uv + 2 v^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,xy,\\,x + y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 2 u^{2} v - 4 uv^{2} + 5 uv + 2 v^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(- 2 u^{2} v - 4 uv^{2} + 5 uv + 2 v^{2})\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533036 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x + y,\\,2 x + y,\\,- z - 1⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=- 2 x^{2} - y^{2}$ for $1\\le x\\le 4$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,- 2 u^{2} - v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 2 u^{2} - v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,- 4 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 2 u^{2} - v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & - 4 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & - 4 u \\\\ 1 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - 4 u \\\\ 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4 u,\\,2 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x + y,\\,2 x + y,\\,- z - 1⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=- 2 u^{2} - v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨u + v,\\,2 u + v,\\,- (- 2 u^{2} - v^{2}) - 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u + v,\\,2 u + v,\\,2 u^{2} + v^{2} - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u + v,\\,2 u + v,\\,2 u^{2} + v^{2} - 1⟩\\cdot ⟨4 u,\\,2 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u + v)\\cdot (4 u)+(2 u + v)\\cdot (2 v)+(2 u^{2} + v^{2} - 1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =4 u^{2} + 4 uv+4 uv + 2 v^{2}+2 u^{2} + v^{2} - 1 \\\\  & =6 u^{2} + 8 uv + 3 v^{2} - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x + y,\\,2 x + y,\\,- z - 1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6 u^{2} + 8 uv + 3 v^{2} - 1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(6 u^{2} + 8 uv + 3 v^{2} - 1)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533048 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3 x - y,\\,- x + 3 y,\\,z + 1⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=- x^{2} - 2 y^{2}$ for $-3\\le x\\le 4$ and $-2\\le y\\le 4.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{-3}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,- u^{2} - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 4,\\,-2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- u^{2} - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,- 2 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- u^{2} - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & - 2 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & - 2 u \\\\ 1 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - 2 u \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 u,\\,4 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨3 x - y,\\,- x + 3 y,\\,z + 1⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=- u^{2} - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- u + 3 v,\\,(- u^{2} - 2 v^{2}) + 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- u + 3 v,\\,- u^{2} - 2 v^{2} + 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- u + 3 v,\\,- u^{2} - 2 v^{2} + 1⟩\\cdot ⟨2 u,\\,4 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3 u - v)\\cdot (2 u)+(- u + 3 v)\\cdot (4 v)+(- u^{2} - 2 v^{2} + 1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =6 u^{2} - 2 uv- 4 uv + 12 v^{2}- u^{2} - 2 v^{2} + 1 \\\\  & =5 u^{2} - 6 uv + 10 v^{2} + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3 x - y,\\,- x + 3 y,\\,z + 1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5 u^{2} - 6 uv + 10 v^{2} + 1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(5 u^{2} - 6 uv + 10 v^{2} + 1)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533051 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x + 2 y,\\,2 x + 3 y,\\,2 z - 2⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=2 x^{2} + y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 4.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2 u^{2} + v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 u \\\\ 1 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 u \\\\ 0 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 4 u,\\,- 2 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x + 2 y,\\,2 x + 3 y,\\,2 z - 2⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2 u^{2} + v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,2 u + 3 v,\\,2 (2 u^{2} + v^{2}) - 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,2 u + 3 v,\\,4 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,2 u + 3 v,\\,4 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2⟩\\cdot ⟨- 4 u,\\,- 2 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u + 2 v)\\cdot (- 4 u)+(2 u + 3 v)\\cdot (- 2 v)+(4 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 4 u^{2} - 8 uv- 4 uv - 6 v^{2}+4 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2 \\\\  & =- 12 uv - 4 v^{2} - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x + 2 y,\\,2 x + 3 y,\\,2 z - 2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 12 uv - 4 v^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(- 12 uv - 4 v^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533059 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3 x - y,\\,- 2 x + y,\\,- z - 2⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 u \\\\ 1 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 u \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 4 u,\\,4 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨3 x - y,\\,- 2 x + y,\\,- z - 2⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- 2 u + v,\\,- (2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}) - 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- 2 u + v,\\,- 2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3 u - v,\\,- 2 u + v,\\,- 2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2⟩\\cdot ⟨- 4 u,\\,4 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3 u - v)\\cdot (- 4 u)+(- 2 u + v)\\cdot (4 v)+(- 2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 12 u^{2} + 4 uv- 8 uv + 4 v^{2}- 2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2} - 2 \\\\  & =- 14 u^{2} - 4 uv + 6 v^{2} - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3 x - y,\\,- 2 x + y,\\,- z - 2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 14 u^{2} - 4 uv + 6 v^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(- 14 u^{2} - 4 uv + 6 v^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533067 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨2 z,\\,xy,\\,x + y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=xy + x - y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 4.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,uv + u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv + u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v + 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv + u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v + 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v + 1 \\\\ 1 & u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & v + 1 \\\\ 0 & u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- v - 1,\\,1 - u,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2 z,\\,xy,\\,x + y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=uv + u - v} \\\\  & =⟨2 (uv + u - v),\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 uv + 2 u - 2 v,\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨2 uv + 2 u - 2 v,\\,uv,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨- v - 1,\\,1 - u,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(2 uv + 2 u - 2 v)\\cdot (- v - 1)+(uv)\\cdot (1 - u)+(u + v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 2 uv^{2} - 4 uv - 2 u + 2 v^{2} + 2 v- u^{2} v + uv+u + v \\\\  & =- u^{2} v - 2 uv^{2} - 3 uv - u + 2 v^{2} + 3 v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2 z,\\,xy,\\,x + y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- u^{2} v - 2 uv^{2} - 3 uv - u + 2 v^{2} + 3 v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}(- u^{2} v - 2 uv^{2} - 3 uv - u + 2 v^{2} + 3 v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533089 :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the triangle spanned by $a=⟨4,-5,-3⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,1,-5⟩.$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot (-5)-(-3)\\cdot 1)i-(4\\cdot (-5)-(-3)\\cdot (-1))j+(4\\cdot 1-(-5)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =28i+23j-k \\\\  & =⟨28,23,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{28}^{2}+{23}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{784+529+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1314} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{146}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{146}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533101 :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the triangle spanned by $p=⟨-3,6,0⟩$ and $q=⟨4,3,-6⟩.$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\times q & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(6\\cdot (-6)-0\\cdot 3)i-((-3)\\cdot (-6)-0\\cdot 4)j+((-3)\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =-36i-18j-33k \\\\  & =⟨-36,-18,-33⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,p\\times q\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-36)^{2}+(-18)^{2}+(-33)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1296+324+1089} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2709} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{301}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{301}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533107 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $r=⟨-5,4,5⟩$ and $t=⟨6,3,-2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\times t & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 3)i-((-5)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 6)j+((-5)\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 6)k \\\\  & =-23i+20j-39k \\\\  & =⟨-23,20,-39⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,r\\times t\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-23)^{2}+{20}^{2}+(-39)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{529+400+1521} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2450} \\\\  & =35\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533110 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $a=⟨-5,4,3⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot (-1))i-((-5)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 1)j+((-5)\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-13i-17j+k \\\\  & =⟨-13,-17,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-13)^{2}+(-17)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169+289+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{459} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{51}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533112 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $r=⟨4,6,1⟩$ and $t=⟨2,6,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\times t & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(6\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot 6)i-(4\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot 2)j+(4\\cdot 6-6\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-36i+22j+12k \\\\  & =⟨-36,22,12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,r\\times t\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-36)^{2}+{22}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1296+484+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1924} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{481}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{481}}{2}=\\sqrt{481}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533116 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $r=⟨5,-5,-4⟩$ and $t=⟨2,1,-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\times t & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 1)i-(5\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 2)j+(5\\cdot 1-(-5)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =9i-3j+15k \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,15⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,r\\times t\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{15}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81+9+225} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{315} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{35}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534002 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}-4}{16-2(7)+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4}{16-14-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-y}-4)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{2x-y-16}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{16-2x+y}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\cancel{16-2x+y}}{\\cancel{(16-2x+y)}(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4} & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534178 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=\\langle 6 t + 4,- \\frac{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{3 t + 2}\\rangle\n$$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](images/db/db3e582e3cbf491015aa1f08ac77ddb6b02b2a8143bebc2d76d171ebd64f9570.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\left\\langle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(6 t + 4),\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- \\frac{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{3 t + 2})\\right\\rangle \\\\ &=\\left\\langle 6,- 3 \\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2}}\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(6)^2+(- 3 \\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2}})^2} \\\\ &=3\\sqrt{4+\\left(\\frac{1}{(3 t + 2)^2}-(3 t + 2)^2\\right)^2} \\\\ &=3\\sqrt{4+\\frac{1}{(3 t + 2)^4}-2+(3 t + 2)^4} \\\\ &=3\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{(3 t + 2)^4}+2+(3 t + 2)^4} \\\\ &=3\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{(3 t + 2)^2}+(3 t + 2)^2\\right)^2} \\\\ &=3 \\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $3 t + 2>0$ throughout the given interval.\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=3 t + 2$, $\\mathrm{d}u=3\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{1}3 \\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\left(3 t + 2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{5}u^{2} + \\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{u^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{u}\\big|_{2}^{5} \\\\ &=1\\left[\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{5^3}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2^3}{3}\\right)\\right] \\\\ &=\\frac{393}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534184 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=\\langle e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)},e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\rangle$ from $t=1$ to $t=2$.\n\n![](images/30/3043adc931d85fe438a292b833ad93e601e50da4ca4c4a1d18d27d932e2e0ccc.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\left\\langle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}),\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)})\\right\\rangle \\\\ &=\\left\\langle - 3 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)},2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(- 3 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})^2+(2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{(2\\cos(3 t)-3\\sin(3 t))^2+(2\\sin(3 t)+3\\cos(3 t))^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{4\\cos(3 t)^2-12\\cos(3 t)\\sin(3 t)+9\\sin(3 t)^2+4\\sin(3 t)^2+12\\cos(3 t)\\sin(3 t)+9\\cos(3 t)^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{13(\\cos^2(3 t)+\\sin^2(3 t))} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{13} e^{2 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{13} e^{2 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{13} \\int_{1}^{2}e^{2 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{13} e^{2 t}}{2}\\big|_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\left(e^{4}-e^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\sqrt{13} \\left(1 - e^{2}\\right) e^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534188 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=\\langle 4 t + 2,- \\frac{\\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{2 t + 1}\\rangle\n$$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](images/8d/8d83a591169c9ac41f6637a985554b0b907fa017349bf92c9197618a3f61409b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\left\\langle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4 t + 2),\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- \\frac{\\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{2 t + 1})\\right\\rangle \\\\ &=\\left\\langle 4,- 2 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2} + \\frac{2}{\\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2}}\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(4)^2+(- 2 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2} + \\frac{2}{\\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2}})^2} \\\\ &=2\\sqrt{4+\\left(\\frac{1}{(2 t + 1)^2}-(2 t + 1)^2\\right)^2} \\\\ &=2\\sqrt{4+\\frac{1}{(2 t + 1)^4}-2+(2 t + 1)^4} \\\\ &=2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{(2 t + 1)^4}+2+(2 t + 1)^4} \\\\ &=2\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{(2 t + 1)^2}+(2 t + 1)^2\\right)^2} \\\\ &=2 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2} + \\frac{2}{\\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2 t + 1>0$ throughout the given interval.\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2 t + 1$, $\\mathrm{d}u=2\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{1}2 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2} + \\frac{2}{\\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}u^{2} + \\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{u^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{u}\\big|_{1}^{3} \\\\ &=1\\left[\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3^3}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{1}+\\frac{1^3}{3}\\right)\\right] \\\\ &=\\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534191 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=\\langle e^{3 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](images/fc/fc465a88c6e529608e8a366c1822b0f8b6e3bde20e30233f25c4a2708369d06f.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\left\\langle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{3 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}),\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\right\\rangle \\\\ &=\\left\\langle - e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 e^{3 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},3 e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + e^{3 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(- e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 e^{3 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(3 e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + e^{3 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=e^{3 t}\\sqrt{(3\\cos(t)-1\\sin(t))^2+(3\\sin(t)+1\\cos(t))^2} \\\\ &=e^{3 t}\\sqrt{9\\cos(t)^2-6\\cos(t)\\sin(t)+1\\sin(t)^2+9\\sin(t)^2+6\\cos(t)\\sin(t)+1\\cos(t)^2} \\\\ &=e^{3 t}\\sqrt{10(\\cos^2(t)+\\sin^2(t))} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10} e^{3 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{10} e^{3 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10} \\int_{0}^{1}e^{3 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{10} e^{3 t}}{3}\\big|_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\left(e^{3}-1\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\sqrt{10} \\left(1 - e^{3}\\right)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534196 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=\\langle e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle$ from $t=0$ to $t=1$.\n\n![](images/68/68033bf43d06f91b46c76c7cc02021269035a64fe610a9513efc847219fbc2dc.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\left\\langle\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}),\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\right\\rangle \\\\ &=\\left\\langle - e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(- e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + e^{2 t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{(2\\cos(t)-1\\sin(t))^2+(2\\sin(t)+1\\cos(t))^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{4\\cos(t)^2-4\\cos(t)\\sin(t)+1\\sin(t)^2+4\\sin(t)^2+4\\cos(t)\\sin(t)+1\\cos(t)^2} \\\\ &=e^{2 t}\\sqrt{5(\\cos^2(t)+\\sin^2(t))} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5} e^{2 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{5} e^{2 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5} \\int_{0}^{1}e^{2 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5} e^{2 t}}{2}\\big|_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left(e^{2}-1\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\sqrt{5} \\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534217 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} & =8\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-7\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(2\\cdot 7-(-6)\\cdot 6)+(-7)((-6)\\cdot 7-(-6)\\cdot (-6))+(-2)((-6)\\cdot 6-2\\cdot (-6)) \\\\  & =8(14-(-36))+(-7)(-42-36)+(-2)(-36-(-12)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 50+(-7)\\cdot (-78)+(-2)\\cdot (-24) \\\\  & =400+546+48 \\\\  & =994\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534218 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =6\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-(-7)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+8\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 7 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6(4\\cdot 6-8\\cdot (-5))+7(7\\cdot 6-8\\cdot 7)+8(7\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 7) \\\\  & =6(24-(-40))+7(42-56)+8(-35-28) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 64+7\\cdot (-14)+8\\cdot (-63) \\\\  & =384-98-504 \\\\  & =-218\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534226 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =(-7)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-7)(7\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 5)+(-2)(7\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 8)+(-2)(7\\cdot 5-7\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =(-7)(-14-15)+(-2)(-14-24)+(-2)(35-56) \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-29)+(-2)\\cdot (-38)+(-2)\\cdot (-21) \\\\  & =203+76+42 \\\\  & =321\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534228 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ -11 & 12 & -7 \\\\ 12 & 7 & 12\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ -11 & 12 & -7 \\\\ 12 & 7 & 12\\end{vmatrix} & =10\\begin{vmatrix}12 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 12\\end{vmatrix}-(-8)\\begin{vmatrix}-11 & -7 \\\\ 12 & 12\\end{vmatrix}+(-9)\\begin{vmatrix}-11 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =10(144-(-49))+8(-132-(-84))+(-9)(-77-144) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 193+8\\cdot (-48)+(-9)\\cdot (-221) \\\\  & =1930-384+1989 \\\\  & =3535\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534229 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-8\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-6)\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(15-48)+(-8)(-21-(-24))+(-6)(42-15) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-33)+(-8)\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =-66-24-162 \\\\  & =-252\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534234 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-8\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)(7\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot (-2))+(-8)(6\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 2)+(-3)(6\\cdot (-2)-7\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(-2)(-28-6)+(-8)(-24-(-6))+(-3)(-12-14) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-34)+(-8)\\cdot (-18)+(-3)\\cdot (-26) \\\\  & =68+144+78 \\\\  & =290\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534237 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{vmatrix} & =(-10)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -7 \\\\ -5 & -12\\end{vmatrix}-(-5)\\begin{vmatrix}-10 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -12\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}-10 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-10)(8\\cdot (-12)-(-7)\\cdot (-5))+5((-10)\\cdot (-12)-(-7)\\cdot (-10))+4((-10)\\cdot (-5)-8\\cdot (-10)) \\\\  & =(-10)(-96-35)+5(120-70)+4(50-(-80)) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot (-131)+5\\cdot 50+4\\cdot 130 \\\\  & =1310+250+520 \\\\  & =2080\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534244 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{vmatrix} & =8\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -9 & -10\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}9 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -10\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(8\\cdot (-10)-(-4)\\cdot (-9))+(-5)(9\\cdot (-10)-(-4)\\cdot (-12))+5(9\\cdot (-9)-8\\cdot (-12)) \\\\  & =8(-80-36)+(-5)(-90-48)+5(-81-(-96)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-116)+(-5)\\cdot (-138)+5\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =-928+690+75 \\\\  & =-163\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534246 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & =7\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 7 \\\\ -12 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-7)\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+9\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -12\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7((-7)\\cdot 5-7\\cdot (-12))+7((-7)\\cdot 5-7\\cdot 12)+9((-7)\\cdot (-12)-(-7)\\cdot 12) \\\\  & =7(-35-(-84))+7(-35-84)+9(84-(-84)) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 49+7\\cdot (-119)+9\\cdot 168 \\\\  & =343-833+1512 \\\\  & =1022\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534251 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =(-9)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-9\\begin{vmatrix}12 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+8\\begin{vmatrix}12 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-9)(8\\cdot 8-(-5)\\cdot 5)+(-9)(12\\cdot 8-(-5)\\cdot 6)+8(12\\cdot 5-8\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =(-9)(64-(-25))+(-9)(96-(-30))+8(60-48) \\\\  & =(-9)\\cdot 89+(-9)\\cdot 126+8\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-801-1134+96 \\\\  & =-1839\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
